<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shah Murad</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1749">1749</a>–<a href="/years/1800">1800</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Shah Murad of Bukhara</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Shah Murad was the first emir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manghit-dynasty">Manghit dynasty</a>, ruling from 1785 until his death in 1800. A pious ruler known for his ascetic life, he set aside the Janid figurehead khans and took the Islamic title of emir. He reformed taxation and coinage, strengthened the religious scholars and campaigned against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merv">Merv</a> and Afghan-held lands south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxus">Amu Darya</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Shah Murad was born in 1749 at Kermine, the eldest of eleven children of Daniyal Biy, a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbek</a> aristocracy who held the post of ataliq. His mother came from the Uzbek Qongirat tribe. His father, who loved him dearly, called him Beg-i Jan, and Bukharan historians also gave him the titles <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commander-of-the-faithful">Amir al-Mu'minin</a>, Commander of the Faithful, Amir Ma'sum Ghazi and Padishah Ghazi. Drawn to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufism</a> from his youth, he spent his days praying in khanaqahs and mosques, and he refused the wealth he inherited, asking that it be shared among the common people.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>His political career began in his father's lifetime, as governor of Kermine and then of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qarshi">Qarshi</a>. In 1780 to 1781 he was made ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a> and worked to restore the city: he built 24 districts and settled many families there from the eastern regions of the country, so that the city grew more populous and its economy and culture improved. A street of Samarkand bears his name. For his modest way of life people called him Amir Ma'sum, the sinless emir.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Coming to power after his father's death in 1785, he launched that year a campaign against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajar</a> principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merv">Merv</a>, meaning to use it as a base for raids on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. He killed its governor, Bayram 'Ali Khan Qajar, conquered the region fully in 1788, and built the town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamabad">Islamabad</a> near Merv. He abolished the luxurious court and set up instead a courtroom of forty judges under his direct supervision, which anyone summoned could attend, high officials and slaves alike; each judge held books written by Shah Murad, which have not survived.</p><p>According to the writer Malcolm, the reverence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbeks</a> allowed him many successful campaigns with an army mainly of cavalry, which he led himself in the plain clothes of a man of religion. He kept diplomatic relations with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>. The historian Ahmad Donish counted him among the renovators of their century in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>. His son Haydar succeeded him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Shah Murad” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1785 <b>Shah Murad</b>, a pious ruler, set aside the Janid figurehead khans and took the title emir, founding the Emirate of Bukhara.</blockquote><a href="/state/emirate-of-bukhara">Emirate of Bukhara</a></li><li><blockquote>The Manghit atalik Muhammad Rahim took effective power, and in 1785 his relative <b>Shah Murad</b> formally founded the Emirate of Bukhara.</blockquote><a href="/state/khanate-of-bukhara">Khanate of Bukhara</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=39.78,64.43&amp;year=1782" aria-label="Bukhara on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6138 740 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Shah Murad"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6588 1010h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6588 1010h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=39.78,64.43&amp;year=1782">What was here in 1782? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
